          <p>
            If you are planning your day around protein, count grams from the label, not scoops. Two products with identical-looking scoops can differ by 10 grams of protein. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> gives the daily target by body weight, and <a href="/how-much-protein-powder-per-day/">how much protein powder per day</a> covers how many scoops make sense inside that target.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more caution on the protein figure. Labels are allowed some tolerance, and independent testing occasionally finds a product short of its claim. A third-party certification such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice checks for banned substances rather than protein content, so it is not a guarantee of grams either, but brands that pay for testing tend to be careful about their panels.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="scoop-vs-serving" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is One Scoop the Same as One Serving?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Usually, but not always: 80 of the 113 products in our database define one serving as one scoop, 23 define it as two scoops, and nine do not print a scoop count at all. If your tub says two scoops per serving, every figure on the panel, from protein to price per serving, assumes you use both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brands split servings for a few reasons. A smaller scoop is easier to dose for a half shake or for baking. Some powders are bulky and would need an awkwardly large scoop. And a two-scoop serving lets the label show a bigger protein number, such as Premier Protein's 30 grams or OWYN Pro Elite's 30 grams, while each scoop alone is closer to 15 grams.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">CHECK THIS FIRST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Read the line under Supplement Facts</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The serving line reads something like "Serving Size 2 Scoops (41 g)". The number in brackets is what matters. Divide the grams by the scoops to know what one scoop of your powder weighs.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            This matters most for cost. A tub that looks cheap per serving can be expensive per scoop if the serving is two scoops, and the other way round. We compare powders by cost per 25 grams of protein for exactly that reason; the method is laid out in <a href="/how-much-does-protein-powder-cost/">how much protein powder costs</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also matters for servings per container. A tub that promises 28 servings will give you about 28 shakes only if you use the labeled serving. Heaping scoops, which most people pour without noticing, can shave three or four shakes off a tub.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tablespoons" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Many Tablespoons Are in a Scoop of Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A typical 30 gram scoop works out to roughly two to four level tablespoons, but there is no single correct conversion because powders differ in density. Fine, dense isolates pack more grams into a spoon; fluffy, aerated or plant powders pack fewer. A measuring spoon measures volume and a label measures weight, and the link between the two changes from tub to tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            The quick way to get your own number takes two minutes. Put a small bowl on a kitchen scale, zero it, and add level tablespoons of your powder one at a time until you reach the labeled serving weight. Count the spoons and write the number on the lid. From then on, the tablespoon is accurate for that product and flavor.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
